    "prose": (
        "Read the source passage below and write exactly two concise, polished "
        "paragraphs for an applied economist. Synthesize its central mechanism, "
        "evidence, limitations, and policy implications. Use only the passage. "
        "Do not use hidden reasoning or a preamble."
    ),
    "stata": (
        "Write complete Stata 19 code for the specification below. Return only a "
        "single do-file in a fenced code block; make it executable, explicit about "
        "variable construction, and robust to missing values. Do not use hidden "
        "reasoning or explanatory prose outside the code."
    ),
}

TASK_SEEDS = {
    "prose": """
The following passage concerns applied empirical economics. Urban economic activity
is shaped by access to jobs, housing costs, transportation networks, environmental
amenities, and local public finance. A change in a transport network can alter the
spatial distribution of employment and households, but the observed correlation
between access and outcomes is not automatically causal. Researchers therefore make
the estimand explicit, define the treatment and comparison populations, and state
which potential outcomes are being compared. A credible design explains why the
counterfactual trend is plausible, how treatment timing is determined, and whether
anticipation, displacement, and changes in composition threaten interpretation.

Difference-in-differences compares changes in treated and comparison units, often
using unit and period fixed effects. Its central identifying restriction is a form
of parallel trends, which must be assessed using pre-treatment outcomes and
institutional knowledge rather than a mechanical significance test alone. Event
studies can display dynamic effects, but leads and lags may be contaminated by
staggered adoption, treatment effect heterogeneity, and noisy estimates. Modern
implementations distinguish the treatment cohort from the calendar period and
aggregate cohort-specific effects transparently. Standard errors should reflect
the level at which treatment varies and the dependence induced by repeated
observations.

Environmental policy creates related measurement problems. Pollution monitors are
not randomly located, exposure may be spatially correlated, and weather affects
both emissions and measured concentrations. Satellite measures can broaden spatial
coverage but introduce retrieval error and missingness. A useful analysis reports
the measurement process, checks sensitivity to alternative samples and bandwidths,
and separates short-run behavioral responses from long-run investment responses.
Distributional effects matter because exposure and adaptation capacity can differ
by income, age, race, tenure, and neighborhood. A policy that improves an average
outcome may still impose costs on a subgroup or shift pollution to an unmeasured
location.

The strongest empirical writing connects institutional detail to the statistical
model. It distinguishes descriptive patterns from causal claims, reports the units
and scale of the estimates, and explains which assumptions are testable and which
are maintained. Robustness checks are informative when they probe a concrete threat
rather than merely producing a longer list of specifications. External validity
requires identifying the population, time period, and policy margin to which the
result is intended to apply. These principles are useful when reading a paper,
report, or long technical memo and deciding whether its mechanism and evidence
support the conclusion.
""".strip(),
